\section{Stress Testing Protocols and Diagnostics}
\label{appendix:stress_tests}

This appendix documents the stress-testing methodology, target metrics,
and diagnostic outputs used to validate the stability and performance
of the Aether-Core/200LY engine.

\subsection{Overview of Stress Test Harness}

The stress test orchestrator coordinates multiple stress vectors:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Particle overload (N-body stress)
    \item Magnetohydrodynamic discontinuity shocks
    \item Memory bandwidth and interconnect latency
\end{itemize}

Each module is executed under controlled chaos injection events to
evaluate fault-tolerance and recovery behavior.

\subsection{Gravitational N-Body Stress}

Synthetic stellar objects are injected into a 200-light-year bounding box
with high spatial crowding:
\begin{equation}
N_{\text{particles}} = 10^8.
\end{equation}

Energy conservation is monitored:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\Delta E}{E} =
\frac{|E_{t+1} - E_t|}{|E_t|}
< 10^{-6}.
\end{equation}

Tree depth stability is recorded as:
\begin{equation}
d_{\text{max}}(t), \quad \bar{d}(t),
\end{equation}
where $d_{\text{max}}$ is the maximum tree depth and $\bar{d}$ is the
average depth per step.

\subsection{MHD Shock Tube Stress}

High-Mach relativistic blast waves are propagated into density
discontinuities in the interstellar medium (ISM). Total variation is
used to detect spurious oscillations:
\begin{equation}
TV(u) = \sum_i |u_{i+1} - u_i|.
\end{equation}

The TVD condition is enforced:
\begin{equation}
TV(u_{\text{num}}) - TV(u_{\text{ref}}) \le \epsilon_{\text{TVD}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Interconnect Latency and Bandwidth}

Multi-GPU halo exchanges are performed across up to 1024 nodes using
GPUDirect MPI. Communication overhead is measured as:
\begin{equation}
\text{Overhead} =
\frac{T_{\text{comm}}}{T_{\text{total}}}
< 0.12.
\end{equation}

Latency spikes induced by chaos events are logged and correlated with
step time deviations.

\subsection{Soft Real-Time Performance Constraint}

Each physics step is subject to a soft real-time bound:
\begin{equation}
t_{\text{step}} \le 0.15 \text{ s}.
\end{equation}

Violations are classified as:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Degraded: $0.15 < t_{\text{step}} \le 0.25$ with recovery
    within a finite number of steps
    \item Unstable: $t_{\text{step}} > 0.25$ or repeated violations
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Chaos Injection Events}

Transient anomalies are introduced at predefined iterations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Iteration 250: simulated GPU memory spike (OOM edge condition)
    \item Iteration 600: simulated network jitter / node drop in MPI halo exchange
\end{itemize}

These events are used to validate:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Checkpoint integrity
    \item Recovery behavior
    \item Persistence of physical anomalies (e.g., 3.55 keV feature)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Health Score Metric}

A global health score is defined as:
\begin{equation}
H = 1 - \alpha N_{\text{perf}} - \beta N_{\text{crit}},
\end{equation}
where $N_{\text{perf}}$ is the number of performance violations and
$N_{\text{crit}}$ is the number of critical failures.

Classification:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Stable: $H > 0.9$
    \item Degraded: $0.7 < H \le 0.9$
    \item Unstable: $H \le 0.7$
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compliance Tagging}

All stress test runs are tagged with:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Scenario ID (e.g., \texttt{AC200LY\_STRESS\_NBODY\_1E8\_v01})
    \item Code version (commit hash)
    \item Hardware profile (GPU model, node count)
\end{itemize}

